#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230032} :activity/id #uuid "ad098e4e-ddaa-5a8c-8676-9f6c2a1554d5" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049727 :topic/id #uuid "0e6d0d09-5c9c-596c-83ce-4f15c32fd909" :topic/math-academy-id 6689} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230032 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230186 :tutorial/content "We know how to apply the trapezoidal method to initial value problems that are linear in $y.$ Can we apply it to more complex initial value problems, such as\n\n$$\ny' = 2x^2 - y^4, \\qquad y(0)=1?\n$$\n\nYes, we can. To do so, we define a function $g$ such that the next $y$-value, $y_\\text{new},$ satisfies the equation $g(y_\\text{new})=0.$ Then, we apply a root-finding method to solve for $y_\\text{new}.$\n\nTo demonstrate, let's apply the trapezoidal method to the IVP above with step size $\\Delta x = 0.5.$\n\nWe begin as usual. In general, the change $\\Delta y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{'}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{'})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2{x}^{2}-{y}^{4}+2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{4})\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =0.5{x}^{2}-0.25{y}^{4}+0.5({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-0.25({y}_{\\text{new}})^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the update formula for $\\Delta y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+0.5{x}^{2}-0.25{y}^{4}+0.5({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-0.25({y}_{\\text{new}})^{4},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand moving all terms to the left-hand side gives the equation\n\n$$\n0.25(y_\\text{new})^4 + y_\\text{new} + 0.25y^4 - y - 0.5x^2 - 0.5(x_\\text{new})^2 = 0.\n$$\n\nHere, there is no simple algebraic rearrangement to isolate $y_\\text{new},$ so solving exactly is impractical.\nInstead, we define a new function $g$ of $y_\\text{new}$ by the expression on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\ng(y_\\text{new}) = 0.25(y_\\text{new})^4 + y_\\text{new} + 0.25y^4 - y - 0.5x^2 - 0.5(x_\\text{new})^2\n$$\n\nNote that $x,$ $y,$ and $x_\\text{new}$ are known constants at each step, so $g$ is truly a one-variable function in $y_\\text{new}.$\n\nThen, the next $y$-value $y_\\text{new}$ is a root of this function: $g(y_\\text{new}) = 0.$ Therefore, to find $y_\\text{new},$ we apply a root-finding method, such as Newton's method.\n\nFor instance, in the first step, the new $x$-value is $x_\\text{new} = 0 + 0.5 = 0.5.$ So, the function used in the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & =0.25({y}_{\\text{new}})^{4}+{y}_{\\text{new}}+0.25\\cdot (1)^{4}-1-0.5\\cdot (0)^{2}-0.5\\cdot (0.5)^{2} \\\\  & =0.25({y}_{\\text{new}})^{4}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-0.875.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we would proceed to use a root-finding method with starting point $y=1$ to solve $g(y_\\text{new})=0.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "9cd9bd37-323b-5d8a-8d1f-4d8924a59da0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14495 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "3c9d3bf2-ca7c-52cb-b895-adf1cf0cbed3" :step/math-academy-id 594484 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230033}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230033 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229156 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228909 :question/id #uuid "9148676f-5899-539c-9b95-4a0d8d225056" :question/math-academy-id "e-21679"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "05af3055-c035-5e11-8946-8791c21a18ac" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048235} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050483}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229655} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229656} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308547} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308550} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308551} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308552} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308553} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308554} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308555} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308572} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377165} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391387}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Function on Which to Apply a Root-Finding Method When Applying the Trapezoidal Method"} :step/id #uuid "33217370-2caf-5db2-a016-cc53daad6665" :step/math-academy-id 594485 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230034}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230034 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229157 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228908 :question/id #uuid "d77c4a1b-863b-57ad-be77-44f2d802f554" :question/math-academy-id "e-21677"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e885f33-5bef-5c95-8afa-495b6512e398" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229654} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229659} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308574} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308614} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308618} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308625} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308626} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308627} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308628} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377186} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377189}] :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using the Trapezoidal Method"} :step/id #uuid "95fba8e6-749f-55be-9019-f637bd866173" :step/math-academy-id 594488}] :activity/title "Using the Trapezoidal Method With Newton's Method" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186229255}]}
